ROMAN_MATHEMATICIANS
====================

TYPE
  Historical period: Roman Republic + Roman Empire
  Dominant style: applied / practical mathematics
  Contrast type: Greek theoretical mathematics (proof-first)

FUNDAMENTALS
  Core value: utility over speculation
  Cicero note: Romans restricted math to measuring and reckoning
  Main uses: land survey, architecture, engineering, tax, administration
  Tools: Roman numerals, abacus, finger counting
  Teaching style: worked examples and procedures, not axiomatic proofs
  Geometry role: rules of thumb for areas, plots, buildings
  Transmission role: later Latin adapters passed Greek math to the Middle Ages

VARIABLES
  Language_of_major_theory: Greek (esp. Alexandria)
  Native_Latin_original_theory: scarce
  Strongest_Roman_output: surveying manuals and applied geometry
  Signature_corpus: Corpus_Agrimensorum_Romanorum
  Everyday_notation: I V X L C D M
  Everyday_calc: abacus + digital finger notation

TYPES_OF_FIGURES
  1. Greek_speaking_scholars_under_Roman_rule
  2. Latin_speaking_practitioners_and_transmitters
  3. Agrimensores / gromatici (professional surveyors)

